window.SBL_IGCSE_PPQ = {"topic": "Rural Fieldwork", "img": "/igcse/geography/learn/past-papers-img/", "lessons": {"IGRF01": {"n": 1, "title": "Our rural enquiry: question, location and the enquiry process", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/rural-fieldwork/l01-our-rural-enquiry"}, "IGRF02": {"n": 2, "title": "Settlement hierarchy: the theory behind our hypothesis", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/rural-fieldwork/l02-settlement-hierarchy-and-hypothesis"}, "IGRF03": {"n": 3, "title": "Planning: types of data, secondary research, equipment and risk", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/rural-fieldwork/l03-data-types-equipment-and-risk"}, "IGRF04": {"n": 4, "title": "Sampling: why and how we sample", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/rural-fieldwork/l04-sampling"}, "IGRF05": {"n": 5, "title": "Rural enquiry methods: how we collected the data", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/rural-fieldwork/l05-data-collection-methods"}, "IGRF06": {"n": 6, "title": "Presenting rural fieldwork data", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/rural-fieldwork/l06-presenting-data"}, "IGRF07": {"n": 7, "title": "Analysis, conclusions and evaluation", "href": "/igcse/geography/learn/rural-fieldwork/l07-analysis-conclusions-evaluation"}}, "parts": [{"key": "P2-2024-5ai", "paper": "2024", "paperLabel": "June 2024 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "text": "State one potential risk that you managed as part of your enquiry.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry. State the title of your geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF03", "ms": "AO3 (1 mark) (1)\n\nAward one mark for a suitable risk:\n\n• Getting lost (1)\n• Falls/slips/injuries (1)\n• Loss of equipment (1)\n• Being hit by traffic/bikes (1)\n• Theft (1)\n• Sunburn/hypothermia (1)\n• Data collected is biased/data not reliable (1)\n• Failure of equipment (1)\n• Difficulty in achieving sample size (1)\n• Issues with wildlife (1)\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2024-5aii", "paper": "2024", "paperLabel": "June 2024 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(a)(ii)", "marks": 2, "text": "Explain one factor that affected the decision on the sites you chose to collect your data.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry. State the title of your geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF04", "ms": "AO3 (2 marks) (2)\n\nAward 1 mark for suitable factor, and a further mark for explanation, up to a maximum of 2 marks.\n\n• Accessibility (1) they needed to be in walking distance to ensure data could be collected (1).\n• To ensure range of data gathered across the village (1) to support reliable conclusions (1).\n• Time available (1) to ensure all sites could be feasibly visited in a day (1).\n• Safety (1) to not go in areas that do not have sufficient pavements (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2024-5b", "paper": "2024", "paperLabel": "June 2024 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(b)", "marks": 2, "text": "Explain one way secondary data was used to support your enquiry.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry. State the title of your geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF03", "ms": "AO3 (2 marks) (2)\n\nAward 1 mark for a suitable way identified, and a further mark for development or explanation.\n\n• Local newspaper articles (1) to provide evidence to support conclusion (1).\n• Government reports (1) provided background data on new transport schemes (1).\n• Government website (1) with data on rural population characteristics (1).\n• Old photographs (1) to show change over time (1).\n• Past investigations (1) to check for annual changes (1).\n• Maps/online maps/land use maps (1) to be able to explore patterns (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n\nSecondary data needs to be specific, do not accept, the internet for information. The secondary data will vary depending on the nature and context of the fieldwork, but it must be plausibly linked to the focus: rural environments.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2024-5c", "paper": "2024", "paperLabel": "June 2024 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(c)", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain one advantage and one disadvantage of a data presentation technique you used in your enquiry.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry. State the title of your geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF06", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks) (4)\n\nAward 1 mark for an advantage and 1 mark for a disadvantage with a further mark for development or explanation up to a maximum of 2 marks each. Max 2 marks for advantage or disadvantages.\n\nThe data presentation techniques will vary depending on the nature and context of the fieldwork, but it must be plausibly linked to the focus: rural environments.\n\nAdvantages:\n• Pie chart allows view of relative proportion of people’s views (1) which allows conclusions to be formed (1).\n• Bar graph give a clear visual picture of the data (1) which provides a summary of questionnaire data to be able to form conclusions (1).\n\nDisadvantages:\n• Pie chart only showed overall proportions of people’s views (1) rather than explaining what they thought.\n• Bar graph only shows a snapshot of information (1) rather than how the data has changed over time (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2024-5d", "paper": "2024", "paperLabel": "June 2024 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(d)", "marks": 3, "text": "Suggest one reason the conclusions from your enquiry were reliable.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry. State the title of your geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF07", "ms": "AO3 (3 marks) (3)\n\nAward 1 mark for an initial point, and further 2 marks for explanation.\n\n• Data was collected at several sites (1) to ensure an accurate representation of views (1) to ensure conclusions could be supported with evidence (1).\n\n• Findings from primary data collection were compared to findings from secondary data collection (1) and similar ideas were found (1) showing how the conclusions confirmed the views about the new development (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2024-5e", "paper": "2024", "paperLabel": "June 2024 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(e)", "marks": 8, "text": "Study Figure 5a and 5b in the Resource Booklet. They show some of the equipment chosen and some of the data collection methods used, including an interview guide. The aim of the student’s enquiry was to investigate local residents’ attitudes to the construction of a new train line through greenfield land on the edge of Berlin, Germany. Evaluate the choice of equipment and the data collection methods used. You must refer to the resources in your answer.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry. State the title of your geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF05", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)/AO4 (4 marks)\nMarking instructions Markers must apply the descriptors in line with the general marking guidance and the qualities outlined in the levels-based mark scheme below.\n\nIndicative content guidance The indicative content below is not prescriptive and candidates are not required to include all of it. Other relevant material not suggested below must also be credited. Relevant points may include:\n\nAO3 ● Evaluation is likely to be linked to the choice of equipment and data collection methods used and how these linked to the aims for the enquiry. ● Evaluation will most likely be linked to how suitable the equipment was for the choice of data collection methods and how this may have affected the conclusions made. ● Recognition of the extent to which aim was met and how the equipment and data collection methods were suitable for the type of fieldwork carried out. ● A supported judgement should be reached about the choice of equipment and data collection methods, such as the strength and weaknesses, or suggestions of additional/alternative equipment or data collection methods which could have been used.\n\nAO4 ● In Figure 5a there shows there is relevant fieldwork equipment for the data collection methods identified (e.g. Interview guide for interviews). ● Figure 5a shows the students are using both primary and secondary data. ● Figure 4a does not specify if they are using any maps. ● In Figure 5b there are no age groups specified in the interview. ● In Figure 5b some of the questions are too general, e.g. Q3. ● In Figure 5b the student only collects quantitative data for Q6 which should have been followed with a question to understand the reasons for their respond in Q6.\n\nLevel Mark Descriptor 0 No acceptable response.\nLevel 1 1–3 ● Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but understanding and connections are flawed. An unbalanced or incomplete argument that provides limited synthesis of understanding. Judgements are supported by limited evidence. (AO3) ● Few aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills to obtain information, which has limited relevance and accuracy. Communicates generic fieldwork findings and uses limited relevant geographical terminology. (AO4)\nLevel 2 4–6 ● Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide some logical connections between concepts. An imbalanced argument that synthesises mostly relevant understanding, but not entirely coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence occasionally. (AO3) ● Some aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills. Communicates fieldwork findings with some clarity, using relevant geographical terminology occasionally. (AO4)\nLevel 3 7–8 ● Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide logical connections between concepts throughout. A balanced, well-developed argument that synthesises relevant understanding coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence throughout. (AO3) ● All aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills. Communicates enquiry-specific fieldwork findings with clarity and uses relevant geographical terminology consistently. (AO4)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2024-q5-p60.jpg", "figs": ["5a", "5b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2023R-5ai", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "text": "Study Figure 5a in the Resource Booklet. Identify one of the open questions in the questionnaire.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF05", "ms": "AO3 (1 mark)\n\nAward one mark for any of the following:\n\n• Question 2/Q2/2 or ‘What benefits does it bring?’ (1)\n• Question 6/Q6/6 or ‘What impact do you think the new farm shop has had on the area? (1) .\n\n(1)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2023R-q5-p65.jpg", "figs": ["5a"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2023R-5aii", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(a)(ii)", "marks": 2, "text": "Explain why closed questions can be useful in a questionnaire.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF05", "ms": "AO3 (2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for initial point, and a further mark for explanation, up to a maximum of 2 marks.\n\n• Closed question allow opinions to be quantified (1) which make data analysis straightforward when creating graphs and charts (1).\n\n• Closed questions are usually quicker to respond to (1) so it might be possible to get more people to answer these (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n\n(2)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2023R-5bi", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(b)(i)", "marks": 2, "text": "Study Figure 5b in the Resource Booklet. It shows environmental quality scores collected at regular distances from the new collection of farm shops. Plot the Environmental Quality Score for 40m and 60m (Figure 5b in the Resource Booklet) to complete the scatter graph below (Figure 5c). Some data has been plotted for you as an example.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "draw", "lesson": "IGRF06", "ms": "AO4 (2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark each correctly plotted point at 40m and 60m.\n\nCorrect diagram shown below: (2)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2023R-q5-p66.jpg", "figs": ["5b"]}, {"src": "p2-2023R-q5-fig5c.jpg", "figs": ["5c"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2023R-5bii", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(b)(ii)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify at which distance there is an anomaly.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF07", "ms": "AO4 (1 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for: 200m (1) Accept 200.\n(2)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2023R-5biii", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(b)(iii)", "marks": 1, "text": "On Figure 5c, draw a line of best fit.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "draw", "lesson": "IGRF07", "ms": "AO4 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for a suitable line drawn, similar to below:\n\n(1)\n\nLine of best fit needs to be within the first and last plots but angle and height can vary slightly from the example shown above.", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2023R-q5-fig5c.jpg", "figs": ["5c"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2023R-5biv", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(b)(iv)", "marks": 2, "text": "Calculate the median Environmental Quality Score (EQS).", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF07", "ms": "AO4 (2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for working and 1 mark for correct answer.\n\n• (52+60) /2 (1) (2)\n\n• 56/56.0 (1)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2023R-5c", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(c)", "marks": 3, "text": "Explain how secondary data could have been used to support this enquiry.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF03", "ms": "AO3 (3 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for a suitable method, and further 2 marks for explanation.\n\n• Local newspaper articles (1) can be used to find out views of local people on the new collection of farm shops (1) and compare to views from questionnaire (1) .\n\n• Reports from local council on rural commerce / tourism (1) to consider potential impacts of the new collection of farm shops (1) and what is being done to minimise impacts (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n(3)\nDo not credit ‘internet’ on its own.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2023R-5d", "paper": "2023R", "paperLabel": "June 2023 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(d)", "marks": 8, "text": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry. State the title of your geographical enquiry Evaluate how far your data collection methods allowed you to achieve your aim.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF07", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)/AO4 (4 marks)\nMarking instructions Markers must apply the descriptors in line with the general marking guidance and the qualities outlined in the levels-based mark scheme below.\n\nIndicative content guidance The indicative content below is not prescriptive and candidates are not required to include all of it. Other relevant material not suggested below must also be credited. Relevant points may include:\n\nAO3 ● Evaluation is likely to be linked to the use of a range of data collection methods to strengthen argument, linked to aims. ● Evaluation will most likely be linked to the evaluation of the data collection methods as well as fieldwork design and the techniques used to analyse and present data. ● Recognition of the extent to which data collection methods chosen were suitable for the data types collected e.g. doesn’t allow for presentation of qualitative data effectively. ● A supported judgement is reached about the data collection methods, drawing on evidence such as strengths, weaknesses, alternatives and relevant examples using data.\n\nAO4 ● There is evidence of using different data collection methods and thought about the design of these methods as part of primary fieldwork. ● There is evidence of using different skills and techniques relevant to design of the methodology stage of the enquiry. ● There is evidence of using different skills and techniques to investigate aspects of rural environments. ● There is evidence of own fieldwork data (and design) i.e. reference to the field data collected by the student.\n\nLevel Mark Descriptor 0 No acceptable response.\nLevel 1 1–3 ● Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but understanding and connections are flawed. An unbalanced or incomplete argument that provides limited synthesis of understanding. Judgements are supported by limited evidence. (AO3) ● Few aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills to obtain information, which has limited relevance and accuracy. Communicates generic fieldwork findings and uses limited relevant geographical terminology. (AO4)\nLevel 2 4–6 ● Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide some logical connections between concepts. An imbalanced argument that synthesises mostly relevant understanding, but not entirely coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence occasionally. (AO3) ● Some aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills. Communicates fieldwork findings with some clarity, using relevant geographical terminology occasionally. (AO4)\nLevel 3 7–8 ● Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide logical connections between concepts throughout.\nA balanced, well-developed argument that synthesises relevant understanding coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence throughout. (AO3) ● All aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills. Communicates enquiry-specific fieldwork findings with clarity and uses relevant geographical terminology consistently. (AO4)\n\n.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2022-5a", "paper": "2022", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(a)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify one question in Figure 5a that would produce qualitative data.", "intro": "Study Figures 5a and 5b in the Resource Booklet. These show extracts from a student’s data collection and presentation methods. The aim of the student’s investigation was to understand how local people felt about the impact of tourists in the Lake District National Park. The student carried out a questionnaire, took photographs of tourist services, and used secondary data about visitor numbers to the national park.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF03", "ms": "AO3 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for:\n\n3 / Why? 4 / How do you think tourists could reduce their impact on the national park?\n\nAccept either the question number on its own or it the candidate has written out the appropriate question shown above. (1)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2022-q5-p57.jpg", "figs": ["5a"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2022-5b", "paper": "2022", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(b)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify one advantage of the presentation type shown in Figure 5b.", "intro": "Study Figures 5a and 5b in the Resource Booklet. These show extracts from a student’s data collection and presentation methods. The aim of the student’s investigation was to understand how local people felt about the impact of tourists in the Lake District National Park. The student carried out a questionnaire, took photographs of tourist services, and used secondary data about visitor numbers to the national park.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF06", "ms": "AO3 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for identifying a suitable advantage.\n\n• Easy to see proportions of data (1)\n• Easy to compare data (1)\n• Clear presentation of results (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response. (1)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2022-q5-p58.jpg", "figs": ["5b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2022-5c", "paper": "2022", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(c)", "marks": 2, "text": "Explain one type of sampling that could have been used in this investigation.", "intro": "Study Figures 5a and 5b in the Resource Booklet. These show extracts from a student’s data collection and presentation methods. The aim of the student’s investigation was to understand how local people felt about the impact of tourists in the Lake District National Park. The student carried out a questionnaire, took photographs of tourist services, and used secondary data about visitor numbers to the national park.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF04", "ms": "AO3 (2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for suitable sampling technique identified and further mark for explanation, up to a maximum of 2 marks.\n\n• Systematic sampling (1) collecting data in an ordered way e.g. every 5th person (1).\n\n• Stratified sampling (1) where a particular number of different categories of people are chosen to reflect the proportion of the number in the total population (1).\n\n• Random sampling (1) where there is no subjectivity over who is chosen to participate (1).\n(2)\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2022-q5-p57.jpg", "figs": ["5a"]}, {"src": "p2-2022-q5-p58.jpg", "figs": ["5b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2022-5d", "paper": "2022", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(d)", "marks": 8, "text": "Study Figures 5a and 5b in the Resource Booklet. Evaluate the student’s data collection and presentation methods.", "intro": "Study Figures 5a and 5b in the Resource Booklet. These show extracts from a student’s data collection and presentation methods. The aim of the student’s investigation was to understand how local people felt about the impact of tourists in the Lake District National Park. The student carried out a questionnaire, took photographs of tourist services, and used secondary data about visitor numbers to the national park.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF07", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)/AO4 (4 marks)\nMarking instructions Markers must apply the descriptors in line with the general marking guidance and the qualities outlined in the levels-based mark scheme below.\n\nIndicative content guidance The indicative content below is not prescriptive and candidates are not required to include all of it. Other relevant material not suggested below must also be credited. Relevant points may include:\n\nAO3 ● Figure 5a shows an appropriate data collection method for exploring views about tourism in the Lake District. ● Figure 5b presents simple data that was produced the questionnaire data, but doesn’t explore the views in detail. ● Figure 5a seems to make it difficult to validate conclusions because there is no way to classify the responses in more detail. ● Figure 5b only shows presentation of data from two questions and doesn’t show in combination with the outcomes of the different methods of the investigation. ● Figure 5b does not present any of the qualitative data that could have revealed the information to help the student reach their aim.\n\nAO4 ● In Figure 5a there are no questions to categorise responses (gender, age etc). ● In Figure 5a there are a range of open and closed questions to generate qualitative and quantitative data. ● In Figure 5a there is no question to check understanding of how people understand the issue of tourism in the Lake District. ● In Figure 5b there are only 2 simple pieces of data presented. ● In Figure 5b there are no data labels for some of the data presented. ● In Figure 5b the pie charts are presented in different sizes.\nLevel Mark Descriptor 0 No acceptable response.\nLevel 1 1–3 ● Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but understanding and connections are flawed. An unbalanced or incomplete argument that provides limited synthesis of understanding. Judgements are supported by limited evidence. (AO3) ● Few aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills to obtain information, which has limited relevance and accuracy. Communicates generic fieldwork findings and uses limited relevant geographical terminology. (AO4)\nLevel 2 4–6 ● Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide some logical connections between concepts. An imbalanced argument that synthesises mostly relevant understanding, but not entirely coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence occasionally. (AO3) ● Some aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills. Communicates fieldwork findings with some clarity, using relevant geographical terminology occasionally. (AO4)\nLevel 3 7–8 ● Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide logical connections between concepts throughout. A balanced, well-developed argument that synthesises relevant understanding coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence throughout. (AO3) ● All aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills. Communicates enquiry-specific fieldwork findings with clarity, and uses relevant geographical terminology consistently. (AO4)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2022-q5-p57.jpg", "figs": ["5a"]}, {"src": "p2-2022-q5-p58.jpg", "figs": ["5b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2022R-5a", "paper": "2022R", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(a)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify one source of secondary data used by the student.", "intro": "Study Figures 5a and 5b in the Resource Booklet. These show extracts from a student's data analysis, conclusions and evaluations about the changing use of rural environments. The aim of the student’s investigation was to investigate local attitudes towards the development of a new visitor centre in The Cévennes National Park, France. The student carried out a questionnaire, environmental quality survey and took photographs, which the student annotated, and collected newspaper articles including photos of the area.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF03", "ms": "AO3 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for:\n\n• Newspaper articles or photos in newspaper articles (1) (1)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2022R-q5-p56.jpg", "figs": ["5a", "5b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2022R-5b", "paper": "2022R", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(b)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify one advantage of using a questionnaire as a data collection method.", "intro": "Study Figures 5a and 5b in the Resource Booklet. These show extracts from a student's data analysis, conclusions and evaluations about the changing use of rural environments. The aim of the student’s investigation was to investigate local attitudes towards the development of a new visitor centre in The Cévennes National Park, France. The student carried out a questionnaire, environmental quality survey and took photographs, which the student annotated, and collected newspaper articles including photos of the area.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF05", "ms": "AO3 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for identifying a suitable advantage.\n\n• Can include qualitative and quantitative data (1)\n• Can allow open and closed questions (1)\n• Easy to gather data (1)\n• Can be easily analysed (1)\n\nAccept any other appropriate response. (1)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2022R-q5-p56.jpg", "figs": ["5a", "5b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2022R-5c", "paper": "2022R", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(c)", "marks": 2, "text": "Suggest one way the reliability of the data could have been affected.", "intro": "Study Figures 5a and 5b in the Resource Booklet. These show extracts from a student's data analysis, conclusions and evaluations about the changing use of rural environments. The aim of the student’s investigation was to investigate local attitudes towards the development of a new visitor centre in The Cévennes National Park, France. The student carried out a questionnaire, environmental quality survey and took photographs, which the student annotated, and collected newspaper articles including photos of the area.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF07", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for a suitable way reliability of data could have been affected and a further mark for extension through explanation, up to a maximum of 2 marks:\n\n• The student might have visited the area at a time when there were not many people around (1) so views collected would not be representative (1).\n\n• The student may have hurried the annotated diagram (1) so missed important details (1).\n\n• The environmental quality survey is subjective (1) so views may differ every time it is conducted (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response. (2)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2022R-q5-p56.jpg", "figs": ["5a", "5b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2022R-5d", "paper": "2022R", "paperLabel": "June 2022 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(d)", "marks": 8, "text": "Study Figures 5a and 5b in the Resource Booklet. Evaluate the student’s data analysis and conclusions.", "intro": "Study Figures 5a and 5b in the Resource Booklet. These show extracts from a student's data analysis, conclusions and evaluations about the changing use of rural environments. The aim of the student’s investigation was to investigate local attitudes towards the development of a new visitor centre in The Cévennes National Park, France. The student carried out a questionnaire, environmental quality survey and took photographs, which the student annotated, and collected newspaper articles including photos of the area.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF07", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)/AO4 (4 marks)\nMarking instructions Markers must apply the descriptors in line with the general marking guidance and the qualities outlined in the levels-based mark scheme below.\n\nIndicative content guidance The indicative content below is not prescriptive and candidates are not required to include all of it. Other relevant material not suggested below must also be credited. Relevant points may include:\n\nAO3 ● Both Figures 5a and 5b do show some outcomes from the fieldwork surveys, therefore it is possible to make some sense of what the student found. ● Figure 5a seems to make it difficult to validate conclusions as not aware of the nature of the data collected to create the word cloud. ● Figure 5b shows three basic conclusions, but it is difficult to use 4a to validate the conclusions as there is not enough detail about where these views were coming from. ● Figure 5b the evaluation statements are quite vague and require more detail, although 4 makes a sensible suggestion about how to improve the study, particularly given conclusion 3.\n\nAO4 ● In Figure 5a there are lots of key words shown in the word cloud. ● In Figure 5a the words are shown in different sizes which may indicate frequency. ● Figure 5a does not provide context of who said these words. ● Figure 5a does not suggest which questions these words respond to. ● In Figure 5b the conclusions are very descriptive. ● In Figure 5b the evaluation statements make suggestions about how the methods could have been improved. ● Figure 5b suggests there were elements of the fieldwork that were not effective.\nLevel Mark Descriptor 0 No acceptable response.\nLevel 1 1–3 ● Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but understanding and connections are flawed. An unbalanced or incomplete argument that provides limited synthesis of understanding. Judgements are supported by limited evidence. (AO3) ● Few aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills to obtain information, which has limited relevance and accuracy. Communicates generic fieldwork findings and uses limited relevant geographical terminology. (AO4)\nLevel 2 4–6 ● Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide some logical connections between concepts. An imbalanced argument that synthesises mostly relevant understanding, but not entirely coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence occasionally. (AO3) ● Some aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills. Communicates fieldwork findings with some clarity, using relevant geographical terminology occasionally. (AO4)\nLevel 3 7–8 ● Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide logical connections between concepts throughout. A balanced, well-developed argument that synthesises relevant understanding coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence throughout. (AO3) ● All aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills. Communicates enquiry-specific fieldwork findings with clarity, and uses relevant geographical terminology consistently. (AO4)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2022R-q5-p56.jpg", "figs": ["5a", "5b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2021-5ai", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "text": "Study Figure 5a in the Resource Booklet. State one piece of secondary data that would be useful to investigate this rural environment.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF03", "ms": "AO3 (1 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for a suitable piece of secondary data.\n\n• Newspaper articles about the area. (1)\n• Local data on rural populations. (1)\n• Census data (1)\n• Leaflet from interested pressure group. (1)\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n(1)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2021-q5-p57.jpg", "figs": ["5a", "5b"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2021-5aii", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(a)(ii)", "marks": 2, "text": "Explain why photographs are a useful source of primary data.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF05", "ms": "AO3 (2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for inital point and a further mark for explanation.\n\n• Can capture lots of detail (1) which means they can be examined properly after the fieldtrip has ended. (1)\n• Provides an accurate representation of the site (1) at that point in time (1).\n• Can be used to capture the state of the site at the present time (1) which can be used to compare to historical photographs. (1).\n• Easy to annotate to show details (1) so can help with analysis (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n\nDo not accept easy to take.\n(2)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2021-5bi", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(b)(i)", "marks": 2, "text": "Study Figure 5c below. It shows data from an environmental quality survey carried out in five sites around a new factory development. Total environmental quality survey score 10 Site 1                                                    Site 2                                                     Site 3                                                    Site 4                                                     Site 5 Site Figure 5c A graph of environmental quality survey data Plot the data for sites 1 and 2 (from Figure 5b in the Resource Booklet) to complete the graph above (Figure 5c). Some data has been plotted for you.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "draw", "lesson": "IGRF06", "ms": "AO4 (1 mark)\n\n1 mark for each correctly plotted bar. Site 1 should be 20 (1) Site 2 should be 15 (1)\n\n(2)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2021-q5-p57.jpg", "figs": ["5a", "5b"]}, {"src": "p2-2021-q5-fig5c.jpg", "figs": ["5c"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2021-5bii", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(b)(ii)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify the type of graph shown in Figure 5c.", "intro": "", "options": [["A", "Bar"], ["B", "Line"], ["C", "Pie"], ["D", "Scatter"]], "answer": "A", "type": "mcq", "lesson": "IGRF06", "ms": "AO4 (1 mark)\n\nA - Bar (1)\n\nAll the other options are incorrect.\n(1)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2021-q5-fig5c.jpg", "figs": ["5c"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2021-5biii", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(b)(iii)", "marks": 1, "text": "Identify the site in Figure 5c that has the highest environmental quality score.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF07", "ms": "AO3 (1 mark)\n\n• Site 4. (1)\n\n(1)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2021-q5-fig5c.jpg", "figs": ["5c"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2021-5biv", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(b)(iv)", "marks": 2, "text": "Calculate the range in environmental quality scores. Show all your workings.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF07", "ms": "AO4 (2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for the calculation of the correct answer = 13\n(1)\nAward 1 mark for correct method to calculate range = 23-10 (1) or working out with similar numbers, i.e. understands idea of difference/substraction (highest and lowest values).\n(2)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2021-5c", "paper": "2021", "paperLabel": "Summer 2021 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(c)", "marks": 3, "text": "Explain one way you could manage a risk during fieldwork in a rural area with a new development.", "intro": "", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF03", "ms": "AO3 (3 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for a correct suggested way to manage a risk, with a further 2 marks for expansion.\n\n• Remain alert (1) to ensure awareness for traffic (1) to prevent accident. (1)\n• Wear appropriate clothing (1) to ensure remain comfortable during fieldwork (1) and reduce chance of illness. (1)\n• Work in groups (1) to ensure safety (1) against unexpected events. (1)\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n\nNote: credit ideas related to risk of poor data collection.\n(3)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019-5ai", "paper": "2019", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "text": "State one type of sampling you used in your geographical enquiry.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry. State the title of your geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF04", "ms": "AO3 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for any of the following.\n\n• Systematic (1)\n\n• Stratified (1)\n\n• Random (1)\n\n• Pragmatic (1)\n\n• Opportunistic (1) (1)\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n\nNote: Title not required for credit.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019-5aii", "paper": "2019", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(a)(ii)", "marks": 2, "text": "Explain one way this sampling technique helped you to collect reliable data or information.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry. State the title of your geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF04", "ms": "AO3 (2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for a reason further mark for extension through explanation, up to a maximum of 2 marks:\n\n• It allowed find out about the spatial variation in the area of study (1) so that we knew that we would get good spatial coverage to minimise bias (1).\n\n• Stratified sampling was used as we had census data about the village population (1) so we could design a fair sampling frame for the questionnaire about energy that we were using (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response, e.g. asking “different people” is allowable. (2)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019-5b", "paper": "2019", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(b)", "marks": 2, "text": "Explain one way you managed a risk associated with your primary data collection.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry. State the title of your geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF03", "ms": "AO3 (2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for identifying a risk and a further expansion mark up to a maximum of 2 marks. Nature of risk, and risk management will vary depending on the location as well as the context of the investigation.\n\n• In the urban area there was lots of traffic so risk the was being struck by a vehicle (1). This was managed by only using a designed crossing (1).\n\n• Fieldwork was carried out in winter so there was a small risk of hypothermia (1). Lots of warm clothes were used (1).\n\n• The wet pavements in the town presented a slip and trip risk. (1). Non-slip shoes were used to minimise the risk of falling over (1).\n\n• I knew that the title focus had published secondary data (1) therefore it was achievable in terms of testing against someone else’s findings – reducing risk (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\nNote – credit ideas related to risk of poor-quality data collection. (2)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019-5c", "paper": "2019", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(c)", "marks": 3, "text": "Draw an annotated sketch map or annotated diagram to show how you selected locations to collect your fieldwork data.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry. State the title of your geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "draw", "lesson": "IGRF04", "ms": "AO3 (3 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for each of the following identifiers:\n\n• Locations that can be identified e.g. places (1)\n• Site numbers along a street / locations of sampling etc (1).\n• Justifications and reasons, developed through explanation and clear geography (2).\n• Scale and direction arrows, titles, key etc (award a max of 1 mark total) (1).\n\nNote: If work does not include annotations or descriptive (3) labels, then max 2 marks.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019-5d", "paper": "2019", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(d)", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain two limitations of the method that you used to collect qualitative data.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry. State the title of your geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF07", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for a method and a further expansion mark, up to a maximum of 2 marks each.\n\nNature of qualitative techniques utilised will vary depending on the location as well as the context of the investigation.\n• Interview data from a number of respondents were used to find out about attitudes towards change in rural areas (1) but it was only a small sample (1) so we could not be convinced about the strength of the conclusions (1).\n\n• Digital photographs were taken of the fieldwork equipment to measure noise (1) so that this could be later used to help evaluate the methods and their reliability (1) but the photos did not include an effective scale (1).\n\n• Field notes recorded aspects of the site location and a description (1). The notes difficult to do since we did not know the history of the area (1) so I was unable to trust their validity (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response, allow questionnaires and EQA / Bi-polar (as opinion) as being qualitative data. (4)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019-5e", "paper": "2019", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2", "q": 5, "id": "5(e)", "marks": 8, "text": "Study Figures 5a, 5b and 5c in the Resource Booklet. They show three different data presentation techniques from a student’s investigation into the changing use of rural environments. The aim of the student’s enquiry was to investigate the attitudes towards the plans for a new tourist development in the New Territories, Hong Kong. The student used three different presentation techniques to help understand people’s opinions towards the proposed tourist development. Evaluate how effective the techniques were in presenting the data and information collected.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry. State the title of your geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF07", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)/AO4 (4 marks)\nMarking instructions Markers must apply the descriptors in line with the general marking guidance and the qualities outlined in the levels-based mark scheme below.\n\nIndicative content guidance The indicative content below is not prescriptive and candidates are not required to include all of it. Other relevant material not suggested below must also be credited. Relevant points may include:\n\nAO3 ● Both Figures 5a and 5b do show some outcomes from the fieldwork surveys, therefore it is possible to make some sense of what the student found. ● Figure 5a seems incomplete as the nature of the questions asked is not present on the key so it’s difficult to draw conclusions. ● Figure 5b only shows one picture, so this might be biased as some could have been missed, therefore this technique is limited in its appropriateness. ● Recognition of issue in presentation (both Figure 5a and 5b) may be flawed in terms of approach and usage. ● An evaluation of how far the presentation techniques can be trusted may be provided.\nAO4 ● In Figure 5a there is no totals column for the Peoples Responses ● In Figure 5a, some rows have incorrect addition ● In Figure 5a the is no strongly agree category ● In Figure 5a the colours may be the wrong choice of plot technique ● In Figure 5b only 2 opinions are shown (there may have been more). ● In Figure 5b the viewer doesn’t know anything about the time the photo was taken, and therefore the frequency of lorry movements etc. ● In Figure 5b don’t anything about the area / context of where the image is taken.\n\nLevel Mark Descriptor 0 No acceptable response.\nLevel 1 1–3 ● Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but understanding and connections are flawed. An unbalanced or incomplete argument that provides limited synthesis of understanding. Judgements are supported by limited evidence. (AO3) ● Few aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills to obtain information, which has limited relevance and accuracy. Communicates generic fieldwork findings and uses limited relevant geographical terminology. (AO4)\nLevel 2 4–6 ● Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide some logical connections between concepts. An imbalanced argument that synthesises mostly relevant understanding, but not entirely coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence occasionally. (AO3) ● Some aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills. Communicates fieldwork findings with some clarity, using relevant geographical terminology occasionally. (AO4)\nLevel 3 7–8 ● Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide logical connections between concepts throughout. A balanced, well-developed argument that synthesises relevant understanding coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence throughout. (AO3) ● All aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills. Communicates enquiry-specific fieldwork findings with clarity, and uses relevant geographical terminology consistently. (AO4)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2019-q5-p61.jpg", "figs": ["5a", "5b"]}, {"src": "p2-2019-q5-p62.jpg", "figs": ["5c"]}]}, {"key": "P2-2019R-5ai", "paper": "2019R", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "text": "State one type of secondary data you used in your geographical enquiry.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF03", "ms": "AO3 (1 mark)\n\nAward 1 mark for any of the following.\n\n• Newspapers (1)\n\n• GIS map / paper map (1)\n\n• Book / magazine (1)\n\n• Government report / policy document (1)\n\n• Blog / forum / social media (1) (1)\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019R-5aii", "paper": "2019R", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(a)(ii)", "marks": 2, "text": "Explain one way this secondary data helped you when investigating rural environments.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF03", "ms": "AO3 (2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for a reason further mark for extension through explanation, up to a maximum of 2 marks:\n\n• It allowed find out about the spatial variation in the area of study (1) so that we knew that we would get good spatial coverage to minimise bias (1).\n\n• Stratified sampling was used as we had census data about the villages population (1) so we could design a fair sampling frame for the questionnaire about energy that we were using (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n(2)\nNote: no credit for just restating the secondary data type.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019R-5b", "paper": "2019R", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(b)", "marks": 2, "text": "State the title of your geographical enquiry. Explain one reason why this title was suitable for your geographical enquiry.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF01", "ms": "AO3 (2 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for identifying a reason and a further expansion mark up to a maximum of 2 marks.\n\nNature of risk, and risk management will vary depending on the location as well as the context of the investigation.\n\n• It was linked to a village close to school (1). Therefore, the data we collected would help answer the initial question set (1).\n\n• The model of rural-urban fringe allowed us to test a theory (1), so the title and focus was established to challenge the assumptions in the model (1).\n\n• The title was both manageable and achievable. (1). Meaning that out primary data could be designed to fully answer the title set (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n\n(2)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019R-5c", "paper": "2019R", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(c)", "marks": 3, "text": "Explain one limitation of a method that you used to collect quantitative data.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF07", "ms": "AO4 (3 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for identification of a reason and a further mark(s) for an explanation of the reason, up to a maximum of 3 marks:\n\n• The EQA scale only goes from -5 to + 5 and it is uncalibrated (1). This means that scores recorded by different people vary (1) which caused inaccuracies in the comparison between sites (1).\n\n• A poor quality closed questions questionnaire used would have potential for errors to be introduced (1) for example by an inappropriate sequencing of questions (1) which would cause inaccurate findings to be recorded (1). (3)\n\nAccept any other appropriate response. Allow secondary as a type of data.", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019R-5d", "paper": "2019R", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(d)", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain two methods you used to analyse some of your fieldwork data.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF07", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)\n\nAward 1 mark for a method and a further expansion mark, up to a maximum of 2 marks each.\n\nNature of qualitative techniques utilised will vary depending on the location as well as the context of the investigation.\n\n• We used quantitative tools such as mean and median (1) so we could final measures of centrality in our data (1).\n\n• Annotated sketches based on photographs from the fieldwork were used (1) and the selected annotations were able to highlight processes operating in the area (1).\n\n• A computer spreadsheet was used to calculate the spread and range in our questionnaire data (1) so that we could compare the outcomes between the two sample sites (1).\n\nAccept any other appropriate response.\n\nNote: allow presentation in the context of analysis.\n\n(4)", "sources": []}, {"key": "P2-2019R-5e", "paper": "2019R", "paperLabel": "June 2019 · Paper 2R", "q": 5, "id": "5(e)", "marks": 8, "text": "Study Figures 5a and 5b in the Resource Booklet. It shows two different data presentation techniques from a student’s fieldwork into the use of rural environments. The aim of the student’s investigation was to identify attitudes towards the plans for a new tourist development in the New Territories, Hong Kong. The student carried out two different types of surveys into people’s opinions and attitudes towards the proposed tourist development. Evaluate the student’s data presentation techniques.", "intro": "You have studied rural environments as part of your own geographical enquiry.", "options": [], "answer": null, "type": "write", "lesson": "IGRF07", "ms": "AO3 (4 marks)/AO4 (4 marks)\nMarking instructions Markers must apply the descriptors in line with the general marking guidance and the qualities outlined in the levels-based mark scheme below.\n\nIndicative content guidance The indicative content below is not prescriptive and candidates are not required to include all of it. Other relevant material not suggested below must also be credited. Relevant points may include:\n\nAO3 ● Both Figures 5a and 5b do show some outcomes from the fieldwork surveys, therefore it is possible to make some sense of what the student found. ● Figure 5a seems incomplete as the nature of the questions asked is not present on the key so it’s difficult to draw conclusions. ● Figure 5b only shows one picture, so this might be biased as some could have been missed, therefore this technique is limited in its appropriateness. ● Recognition of issue in presentation (both Figure 5a and 5b) may be flawed in terms of approach and usage. ● An evaluation of how far the presentation techniques can be trusted may be provided.\nAO4 ● In Figure 5a there is no totals column for the Peoples Responses ● In Figure 5a the is no strongly agree category ● In Figure 5a the colours may be the wrong choice of plot technique ● In Figure 5b only 2 opinions are shown (there may have been more). They may have been edited. ● In Figure 5b there is no idea as to how the responses were selected ● In Figure 5b show anything about the area / context of where the interview took place and when.\n\nLevel Mark Descriptor 0 No acceptable response.\nLevel 1 1–3 ● Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but understanding and connections are flawed. An unbalanced or incomplete argument that provides limited synthesis of understanding. Judgements are supported by limited evidence. (AO3) ● Few aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills to obtain information, which has limited relevance and accuracy. Communicates generic fieldwork findings and uses limited relevant geographical terminology. (AO4)\nLevel 2 4–6 ● Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide some logical connections between concepts. An imbalanced argument that synthesises mostly relevant understanding, but not entirely coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence occasionally. (AO3) ● Some aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills. Communicates fieldwork findings with some clarity, using relevant geographical terminology occasionally. (AO4)\nLevel 3 7–8 ● Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide logical connections between concepts throughout. A balanced, well-developed argument that synthesises relevant understanding coherently, leading to judgements that are supported by evidence throughout. (AO3) ● All aspects of the enquiry process are supported by the use of geographical skills. Communicates enquiry-specific fieldwork findings with clarity, and uses relevant geographical terminology consistently. (AO4)", "sources": [{"src": "p2-2019R-q5-p56.jpg", "figs": ["5a", "5b"]}]}]};
